===Etymology===
From early modern {{etyl|nl|de}} {{term|frikadel|lang=nl}}, which is borrowed from {{etyl|fr|de}} {{term|fricadel|lang=fr}}, an only later attested variant of {{term|fricandeau|lang=fr}}. A further relation with {{etyl|it|-}} {{term|frittatella||lang=it}} is speculative. Compare contemporary {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|frikandel|lang=nl}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˌfʁɪkaˈdɛlə/|/ˌfʁiːka-/|lang=de}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|f||Frikadellen}}

# {{context|regional|lang=de}} {{l|en|meatball}}

====Usage notes====
* ''Frikadelle'' is the most widely accepted word for “meatball” in German. It is nevertheless regionally bound and heard most often in central-western and north-western Germany.

====Declension====
{{de-decl-noun-f|n}}

